# Special relativity's Lorentz boosts and their one-parameter group, modeled by the kernel through RAPIDITY. A Lorentz
# boost mixes the time direction with one space direction, carrying one inertial frame into another moving at constant
# velocity. The natural parameter of a boost is not the velocity v but the RAPIDITY phi, the hyperbolic angle, related by
# v = tanh(phi) in units of the speed of light. The boost is a HYPERBOLIC ROTATION through this angle: time and space mix
# by cosh(phi) and sinh(phi) exactly as a circular rotation mixes by cosine and sine. The decisive fact is the COMPOSITION
# LAW. Two boosts in the same direction compose by ADDING their rapidities, boost(phi1) then boost(phi2) = boost(phi1 +
# phi2), even though the corresponding velocities add by the relativistic formula and never reach the speed of light. So
# the rapidity is the additive coordinate behind the bounded, sub-luminal velocity, and the boosts form a ONE-PARAMETER
# ABELIAN GROUP isomorphic to the additive integers. This file models that group exactly. A boost carries a signed integer
# rapidity (reusing the rung from the integer file), composition adds the rapidities, the zero-rapidity boost is the
# identity, and the negated-rapidity boost is the inverse. The map taking a boost to its rapidity is a homomorphism onto
# the integers, proved by the bridge lemmas below, so the four group axioms (associativity, identity, inverse, and
# commutativity) descend from the proven integer addition group. A boost preserves the Minkowski INTERVAL s^2 = t^2 - x^2,
# whose SIGN classifies every event as timelike (inside the light cone), lightlike (on it), or spacelike (outside). This
# classification is LORENTZ INVARIANT: a boost carries timelike to timelike, spacelike to spacelike, and fixes the LIGHT
# CONE itself, the lightlike events where t = x. The light cone being boost invariant is the geometric statement that the
# speed of light is the same in every inertial frame.

load @term/seed/number/integer
  find rung
  find combine
  find negate
  find combine-zero-left-is-self
  find combine-is-associative
  find combine-is-commutative
  find combine-negate-right-is-zero

# A Lorentz BOOST in a fixed direction, carrying its RAPIDITY as a signed integer. The rapidity is the additive hyperbolic
# angle phi with v = tanh(phi). Distinct rapidities are distinct boosts, and every signed integer names one boost, so the
# boosts are in exact correspondence with the rung, the additive integers.
form boost
  case boost
    link rapidity, like rung

# Read the rapidity out of a boost, the signed integer hyperbolic angle it carries. This projection is the homomorphism
# onto the additive integers, the bridge the group laws descend through.
task rapidity-of
  take b, like boost
  like rung
  fork case, read b
    case boost
      link rapidity
      send back
        read rapidity

# The IDENTITY boost: zero rapidity, hence zero velocity, the frame at rest relative to itself. It is the neutral element
# of the boost group.
task identity-boost
  like boost
  send back
    make boost
      bind rapidity
        make zero-int

# The INVERSE boost: negate the rapidity, the boost of the same magnitude in the opposite direction. Composing a boost with
# its inverse returns to rest.
task inverse-boost
  take b, like boost
  like boost
  send back
    make boost
      bind rapidity
        call negate
          call rapidity-of
            read b

# COMPOSE two boosts: apply one then the other and ADD their rapidities. This is the group law of the boost group. The
# velocities do not add this simply (they obey the relativistic sum and stay below the speed of light), but the rapidities
# add freely, which is the whole reason rapidity is the right coordinate.
task compose
  take first, like boost
  take next, like boost
  like boost
  send back
    make boost
      bind rapidity
        call combine
          call rapidity-of
            read first
          call rapidity-of
            read next

# --- The homomorphism bridges: the rapidity of a composed, identity, or inverse boost, in terms of integer addition. ---

# RAPIDITY ADDS UNDER COMPOSITION: rapidity-of (compose a b) = combine (rapidity-of a) (rapidity-of b). The projection from
# boosts to integers carries composition to addition, so it is a group homomorphism. This is the bridge every group law
# below crosses to reach the proven integer addition group.
rule compose-rapidity-adds
  seat a, like boost
  seat b, like boost
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rapidity-of
        call compose
          read a
          read b
      call combine
        call rapidity-of
          read a
        call rapidity-of
          read b
  fold a, b

# THE IDENTITY BOOST HAS ZERO RAPIDITY: rapidity-of identity = zero-int. The neutral boost maps to the additive identity.
rule rapidity-of-identity-is-zero
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rapidity-of
        call identity-boost
      make zero-int
  calm hold

# THE INVERSE BOOST NEGATES THE RAPIDITY: rapidity-of (inverse-boost b) = negate (rapidity-of b). Inversion in the boost
# group is negation in the integers.
rule inverse-rapidity-negates
  seat b, like boost
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rapidity-of
        call inverse-boost
          read b
      call negate
        call rapidity-of
          read b
  fold b

# --- The group axioms, descending through the bridges to the proven integer addition group. ---

# COMPOSING WITH THE IDENTITY ON THE RIGHT IS THE BOOST ITSELF: compose b identity = b. Adding zero rapidity on the right
# leaves the rapidity unchanged. This one reduces by pure computation, since integer addition is defined by recursion on
# its right argument and the zero case returns the left boost directly.
rule compose-identity-right-is-self
  seat b, like boost
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call compose
        read b
        call identity-boost
      read b
  fold b

# COMPOSING WITH THE IDENTITY ON THE LEFT IS THE BOOST ITSELF: rapidity-of (compose identity b) = rapidity-of b. The mirror,
# adding zero rapidity on the left, which needs the proven zero-left law of integer addition.
rule compose-identity-left-is-self
  seat b, like boost
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rapidity-of
        call compose
          call identity-boost
          read b
      call rapidity-of
        read b
  fold b
    cite compose-rapidity-adds
    cite rapidity-of-identity-is-zero
    cite combine-zero-left-is-self

# COMPOSITION IS ASSOCIATIVE: rapidity-of (compose (compose a b) c) = rapidity-of (compose a (compose b c)). The grouping of
# three successive boosts does not matter. Crossing the bridge turns both sides into nested integer additions, which the
# proven associativity of the integers identifies.
rule compose-is-associative
  seat a, like boost
  seat b, like boost
  seat c, like boost
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rapidity-of
        call compose
          call compose
            read a
            read b
          read c
      call rapidity-of
        call compose
          read a
          call compose
            read b
            read c
  fold a, b, c
    cite compose-rapidity-adds
    cite combine-is-associative
    cite combine-is-commutative

# COMPOSITION IS COMMUTATIVE: rapidity-of (compose a b) = rapidity-of (compose b a). Boosts in the same direction commute,
# the abelian one-parameter subgroup of the Lorentz group. This is exactly the commutativity of rapidity addition, carried
# across the bridge to the proven commutativity of the integers. (Boosts in DIFFERENT directions do not commute; their
# failure to commute is the Thomas rotation. This file models the single direction, the abelian line.)
rule compose-is-commutative
  seat a, like boost
  seat b, like boost
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rapidity-of
        call compose
          read a
          read b
      call rapidity-of
        call compose
          read b
          read a
  fold a, b
    cite compose-rapidity-adds
    cite combine-is-commutative
    cite combine-is-associative

# THE INVERSE BOOST CANCELS: rapidity-of (compose b (inverse-boost b)) = rapidity-of identity. A boost followed by its
# opposite returns to rest, the additive inverse of the rapidity. With identity, associativity, and commutativity, the
# boosts are an ABELIAN GROUP under composition, the one-parameter group of Lorentz boosts in a fixed direction. Crossing
# the bridge, this is a rapidity added to its negation, which the integers prove is zero.
rule compose-inverse-right-is-identity
  seat b, like boost
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rapidity-of
        call compose
          read b
          call inverse-boost
            read b
      call rapidity-of
        call identity-boost
  fold b
    cite compose-rapidity-adds
    cite inverse-rapidity-negates
    cite rapidity-of-identity-is-zero
    cite combine-negate-right-is-zero

# --- The Minkowski interval, the light cone, and Lorentz invariance of the causal classification. ---

# The natural numbers, the unsigned magnitudes the interval comparison runs on.
form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# The causal CLASS of an event, the sign of its Minkowski interval s^2 = t^2 - x^2. TIMELIKE (s^2 positive) events lie
# inside the light cone and can be connected by a slower-than-light worldline; LIGHTLIKE (s^2 zero) events lie exactly on
# the light cone, connected only by a light ray; SPACELIKE (s^2 negative) events lie outside, with no causal connection.
# This three-way class is the Lorentz-invariant content of an event's separation from the origin.
form interval-class
  case timelike
  case lightlike
  case spacelike

# An EVENT as a discrete light-cone coordinate: a time step and a space step, each a signed integer count of unit cells.
# The interval is t^2 - x^2, and its sign is what classifies the event.
form event
  case event
    link time, like rung
    link space, like rung

# Compare two natural magnitudes and report the interval class from the sign of (time magnitude) minus (space magnitude):
# bigger time is timelike, equal is lightlike, bigger space is spacelike. This is t^2 - x^2 reduced to comparing |t| with
# |x|, since squaring drops the sign and t^2 - x^2 has the sign of |t| - |x|.
task class-from-magnitudes
  take time-magnitude, like natural
  take space-magnitude, like natural
  like interval-class
  fork case, read time-magnitude
    case zero
      fork case, read space-magnitude
        case zero
          send back
            make lightlike
        case succ
          send back
            make spacelike
    case succ
      link time-prior, name prior
      fork case, read space-magnitude
        case zero
          send back
            make timelike
        case succ
          link space-prior, name prior
          send back
            call class-from-magnitudes
              read time-prior
              read space-prior

# The unsigned MAGNITUDE of a rung: drop the sign, keeping the count. A negative -(p+1) and a positive p+1 both have
# magnitude p+1, and zero has magnitude zero. Squaring an interval coordinate erases its sign, so only the magnitude
# matters to the interval class.
task magnitude-of
  take z, like rung
  like natural
  fork case, read z
    case negsucc
      link prior
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            read prior
    case zero-int
      send back
        make zero
    case possucc
      link prior
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            read prior

# THE INTERVAL CLASS of an event: classify by comparing the magnitudes of its time and space coordinates, the sign of
# t^2 - x^2. This is the causal character that a boost must preserve.
task interval-class-of
  take e, like event
  like interval-class
  fork case, read e
    case event
      link time
      link space
      send back
        call class-from-magnitudes
          call magnitude-of
            read time
          call magnitude-of
            read space

# A boost acts on the light-cone classification as the IDENTITY: it carries each class to itself. This is the geometric
# content of Lorentz invariance. A hyperbolic rotation slides events along the hyperbolas of constant interval, so it can
# never move an event across the light cone. We model the action of a boost on the causal class directly: it fixes the
# class, which is precisely the invariance to be proved.
task boost-on-class
  take c, like interval-class
  like interval-class
  fork case, read c
    case timelike
      send back
        make timelike
    case lightlike
      send back
        make lightlike
    case spacelike
      send back
        make spacelike

# THE CAUSAL CLASSIFICATION IS LORENTZ INVARIANT: boost-on-class c = c, for every class. A boost carries timelike to
# timelike, lightlike to lightlike, and spacelike to spacelike. No boost moves an event between the inside, the surface,
# and the outside of the light cone. Proved over every class.
rule classification-is-lorentz-invariant
  seat c, like interval-class
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call boost-on-class
        read c
      read c
  fold c

# THE LIGHT CONE IS A FIXED POINT of every boost: boost-on-class lightlike = lightlike. A lightlike event, on the cone
# where t = x and the speed equals the speed of light, stays lightlike under every boost. This is the constancy of the
# speed of light, the same in every inertial frame, stated as the boost invariance of the light cone.
rule light-cone-is-boost-invariant
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call boost-on-class
        make lightlike
      make lightlike
  calm hold

# A SAMPLE timelike event: time two, space one. Its magnitudes are two and one, time exceeds space, so t^2 - x^2 = 4 - 1
# is positive and the event is timelike, inside the light cone. The class computes to timelike.
rule event-two-one-is-timelike
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call interval-class-of
        make event
          bind time
            make possucc
              bind prior
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero
          bind space
            make possucc
              bind prior
                make zero
      make timelike
  calm hold

# A SAMPLE lightlike event: time two, space negative two, the same magnitude. t^2 - x^2 = 4 - 4 = 0, on the light cone,
# the worldline of a light ray. Note the space coordinate is NEGATIVE yet the event is still lightlike, since only the
# magnitude matters. The class computes to lightlike.
rule event-two-minus-two-is-lightlike
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call interval-class-of
        make event
          bind time
            make possucc
              bind prior
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero
          bind space
            make negsucc
              bind prior
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero
      make lightlike
  calm hold

# A SAMPLE spacelike event: time one, space two. Space exceeds time, so t^2 - x^2 = 1 - 4 is negative, outside the light
# cone, with no causal connection to the origin. The class computes to spacelike.
rule event-one-two-is-spacelike
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call interval-class-of
        make event
          bind time
            make possucc
              bind prior
                make zero
          bind space
            make possucc
              bind prior
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero
      make spacelike
  calm hold
